From 204644a26891b2ea89ee7f7a322073e3bf754224 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Vuln Patch Pipeline <vuln-patch@chainguard.dev>
Date: Mon, 9 Nov 2026 17:07:19 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] Require a signed JWT for token pre-authentication over HTTPS

TokenPreauth.verify() guarded its signature check with
"if (!tokenDecoder.isSigned() && !kdcRequest.isHttps())". For a request
marked as HTTPS the check was skipped, so a forged, unsigned alg=none
JWT, or an encrypted JWT wrapping unsigned claims, carrying an arbitrary
subject was accepted, the token was set on the request, and the KDC
would issue a ticket for the named principal (CWE-347, related CWE-287
and CWE-345). TLS on the transport does not prove the client's identity
and must not stand in for a verified token signature.

The isHttps() term is removed, so a token that is not signed is refused
with "Token should be signed." on every request. The token decoder, key
loading, the HTTPS and armor-key decoding of PA-TOKEN-REQUEST, the
issuer and audience checks, and requests not marked as HTTPS are
unchanged. Signed tokens from a configured issuer are still accepted.
HAS hands the KDC HTTPS-marked requests carrying unsigned tokens, so
those now fail until the tokens are signed.

Adds TokenPreauthHttpsSignatureBypassTest, which drives a real
TokenPreauth and KdcContext with an HTTPS-marked request and asserts
that an unsigned PlainJWT and an EncryptedJWT wrapping unsigned claims
are refused before any token is attached, and that an RSA-signed JWT
still authenticates with its subject attached.

Cgp-Finding: CGP-3x2p-3qw2-2ghh
